💡 Key Exploits and Mitigation Strategies

I successfully identified, exploited, and documented the risk and remediation for each of the major vulnerabilities.

| OWASP Top 10 Category | Exploitation Method Demonstrated | Mitigation Strategy (Blue Team Takeaway) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| A01:2021: Broken Access Control | Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) by manipulating user IDs in URL parameters to access other users' accounts/data. | Implement Principle of Least Privilege (PoLP) and use session-level authorization checks for every request accessing a resource. |
| A03:2021: Injection | SQL Injection via input fields, bypassing login, and extracting database contents. | Always use Parameterized Queries (Prepared Statements) to separate data from query logic, and implement strict input validation. |
| A04:2021: Insecure Design | Business Logic Flaws where application trust was placed solely on client-side input, allowing unauthorized actions. | Integrate Threat Modeling into the Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) to proactively design security controls. |
| A05:2021: Security Misconfiguration | Exploited default credentials, exposed administrative interfaces, and non-hardened configurations (e.g., verbose error messages). | Implement a Hardening Checklist (CIS Benchmarks) and ensure all default configurations and credentials are changed/disabled upon deployment. |
| A07:2021: Identification and Authentication Failures | Circumvented weak password policies or exploited lack of rate-limiting on login forms (Brute-Forcing). | Enforce strong, complex passwords, implement MFA, and establish effective rate limiting on all authentication endpoints. |
| A09:2021: Security Logging and Monitoring Failures | Executed attacks without generating adequate log data, preventing detection and analysis. | Ensure comprehensive, normalized logs capture all critical events (e.g., failed logins, object access, command execution) and are fed to a SIEM. |

